    "<entry>",
    "<id>tag:github.com,2008:Grit::Commit/aeef73477fd1b0a03291fd9c0280b32208b45976</id>",
    "<link type=\"text/html\" rel=\"alternate\" href=\"https://github.com/YukonWRB/AquaCache/commit/aeef73477fd1b0a03291fd9c0280b32208b45976\"/>",
    "<title>",
    "patch 60: exclude both owners from both sides of the privilege check",
    "</title>",
    "<updated>2026-09-08T17:15:23Z</updated>",
    "<media:thumbnail height=\"30\" width=\"30\" url=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/132517733?s=30&amp;v=4\"/>",
    "<author>",
    "<name>EmmaGRiley</name>",
    "<uri>https://github.com/EmmaGRiley</uri>",
    "</author>",
    "<content type=\"html\">",
    "&lt;pre style=&#39;white-space:pre-wrap;width:81ex&#39;&gt;patch 60: exclude both owners from both sides of the privilege check",
    "table_privilege_differences() excluded each table&amp;#39;s own owner from its own side of the comparison. That is only sound when parent and child share an owner. This patch creates its tables OWNER TO admin, while discrete.samples and discrete.results inherited from an older restore are owned by postgres with admin holding an explicit GRANT SELECT. admin is therefore kept on the source side as a plain grantee and filtered out on the target side as owner, so its parent grant is reported missing on a table it owns outright.",
    "The result is a false positive that blocks patch 60 entirely on any database whose discrete tables predate the OWNER TO admin convention. Effective privileges were correct throughout: every other grantee matched on both sides, so the grant propagation the check exists to verify was working.",
    "Excluding both tables&amp;#39; owners from both sides keeps the intent - a child must inherit its parent&amp;#39;s explicit grants for every role that is not an owner of either table - without demanding an explicit grant from a role that needs none. inherit_table_privileges() is deliberately left alone; excluding the source owner there is correct.&lt;/pre&gt;",
    "</content>",
    "</entry>",
    "<entry>",
    "<id>tag:github.com,2008:Grit::Commit/7a46935850fc4bbea9ea973456614d2786da9983</id>",
    "<link type=\"text/html\" rel=\"alternate\" href=\"https://github.com/YukonWRB/AquaCache/commit/7a46935850fc4bbea9ea973456614d2786da9983\"/>",
    "<title>",
    "patch 60: add the aggregate-to-direct conversion path and its delete …",
    "</title>",
    "<updated>2026-09-08T17:15:23Z</updated>",
    "<media:thumbnail height=\"30\" width=\"30\" url=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/132517733?s=30&amp;v=4\"/>",
    "<author>",
    "<name>EmmaGRiley</name>",
    "<uri>https://github.com/EmmaGRiley</uri>",
    "</author>",
    "<content type=\"html\">",
    "&lt;pre style=&#39;white-space:pre-wrap;width:81ex&#39;&gt;patch 60: add the aggregate-to-direct conversion path and its delete guard",
    "Adds discrete.convert_result_aggregation_to_direct() with modes preserve_calculated and replace, and re-adds forbid_result_aggregation_delete_trigger, which it makes safe. Sanctioned deletes announce themselves with transaction-local aquacache.allow_result_aggregation_delete, the same idiom as aquacache.defer_result_aggregation_refresh. The cascade from discrete.results is still permitted.",
    "The reason is preserved in the audit trail by writing it onto result_aggregations.note immediately before the delete, so the existing generic audit trigger captures it in new_data of the UPDATE and original_data of the DELETE. No change to audit.if_modified_func().",
    "synchronize_discrete_sample_detail() now converts through the function instead of issuing a raw DELETE, and brackets its in-place rebuild deletes with the guard variable. Rebuild-in-place and convert are treated as two separate sanctioned operations.",
    "Deviates from step 6 of the spec in the file header: IMMEDIATE is restored only when the function deferred the constraints itself. SET CONSTRAINTS ... IMMEDIATE fires every outstanding deferred check in the transaction, not only this function&amp;#39;s, so restoring unconditionally breaks the sole caller, which inserts new aggregate parent results with a NULL result before their components exist. set_result_aggregation_constraints() now records the mode transaction-locally so the function can tell who owns it.&lt;/pre&gt;",
    "</content>",
    "</entry>",
    "<entry>",
    "<id>tag:github.com,2008:Grit::Commit/4a1f547417e38b4e4ba9b632bbd9af73b19ea45d</id>",
    "<link type=\"text/html\" rel=\"alternate\" href=\"https://github.com/YukonWRB/AquaCache/commit/4a1f547417e38b4e4ba9b632bbd9af73b19ea45d\"/>",
    "<title>",
    "patch 59: scope sub_locations_name_key to (location_id, sub_location_…",
    "</title>",
    "<updated>2026-09-08T17:15:23Z</updated>",
    "<media:thumbnail height=\"30\" width=\"30\" url=\"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/132517733?s=30&amp;v=4\"/>",
    "<author>",
    "<name>EmmaGRiley</name>",
    "<uri>https://github.com/EmmaGRiley</uri>",
    "</author>",
    "<content type=\"html\">",
    "&lt;pre style=&#39;white-space:pre-wrap;width:81ex&#39;&gt;patch 59: scope sub_locations_name_key to (location_id, sub_location_name)",
    "sub_locations.location_id is NOT NULL, so a sub-location name is only meaningful within its parent location, and the same name recurring under different locations is normal. Unique on the name alone blocks patch 59 on any database where two locations share a sub-location name.",
    "Found applying patch 59 to eccdb, where NWT snow has 10,258 sub_locations across 203 transects, each numbering Point 1-10 and Extra depth 1-50. The branch fixture holds only a few sub_locations with distinct names, so testdb cannot reach this.&lt;/pre&gt;",
    "</content>",
    "</entry>",
    "<entry>",
    "<id>tag:github.com,2008:Grit::Commit/597afc1dbd3324662f5569fe7cd0fc1c9f555635</id>",
